  <h1>India’s Defence Indigenisation — Progress under Atmanirbhar Bharat</h1>
  <div><em>Economic Times | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>India is among the world’s largest arms importers, but recent years have seen a push for defence indigenisation under the Atmanirbhar Bharat vision. In 2024, Economic Times reviewed progress in local manufacturing, noting achievements in exports, policy reforms, and industry participation. Defence indigenisation is a crucial UPSC theme linking security, economy, and technology.
________________________________________
Current Defence Scenario
•	Imports: Still account for ~40% of defence needs.
•	Indigenous Content: Increased from ~40% (2015) to ~55% (2024).
•	Exports: Record ₹21,000 crore in 2023–24, with buyers in 80+ countries.
•	Major Projects: Tejas LCA, Akash SAM, INS Vikrant (aircraft carrier), Pinaka rockets, BrahMos exports.
________________________________________
Policy Reforms
1.	Positive Indigenisation Lists: 400+ items banned from imports, to be sourced domestically.
2.	Defence Acquisition Procedure (DAP 2020): Prioritizes ‘Buy Indian’ category.
3.	FDI Liberalisation: 74% automatic route in defence manufacturing.
4.	Innovation Push: iDEX (Innovations for Defence Excellence) supports startups and MSMEs.
5.	Corporatisation of Ordnance Factories: 7 new DPSUs for efficiency.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Achievements<br/>•	Private Sector Entry: Tata, L&T, Adani, Bharat Forge in defence manufacturing.<br/>•	Export Growth: From ₹1,500 crore in 2015 to ₹21,000 crore in 2024.<br/>•	Technology Transfer: Partnerships with US, France, Israel, Russia.<br/>•	Navy Leadership: Indigenous ships and submarines under “Make in India.”<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>1.	Technology Dependence: Reliance on foreign engines, sensors, avionics.<br/>2.	R&D Deficit: DRDO projects often delayed, with cost overruns.<br/>3.	Quality & Reliability Issues: Domestic products not always on par with imports.<br/>4.	Private–Public Gaps: MSMEs face financial and scale barriers.<br/>5.	Export Constraints: Competition from established global players.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Strategic Significance<br/>•	National Security: Reduces dependence on foreign suppliers during crises.<br/>•	Economic Growth: Defence sector as driver of manufacturing and jobs.<br/>•	Global Power Status: Defence exports strengthen diplomacy.<br/>•	Self-Reliance: Boosts credibility of India as a defence partner.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-III (Security): Indigenisation, Atmanirbhar Bharat, defence policy.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Reforms, PPPs in defence.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Self-reliance in defence is the bedrock of sovereignty.”<br/>o	“India’s rise as a power depends on technological autonomy.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Defence industrial base as state capacity.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Boost R&D: Raise defence R&D to 2.5% of defence budget; incentivize private innovation.<br/>2.	Strengthen PPP: Encourage private–public partnerships in complex systems.<br/>3.	Skill Development: Create talent pipelines in AI, robotics, aerospace.<br/>4.	Global Collaborations: Joint ventures with tech-sharing clauses.<br/>5.	Export Ecosystem: Fast-track clearances, credit lines, marketing support.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s defence indigenisation is on the right trajectory but requires technology depth, strong R&D, and synergy between state and private actors to succeed. For UPSC aspirants, it exemplifies how security, economy, and technology policy converge in nation-building.</p>
